  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date the Department of the Interior has obligated $4.1B on contracts. Obligations fell 37.9% year over year, with 18,405 awards and an average award size of $220K. The agency's recent fiscal history shows higher totals in prior years: FY21 was $5.1B, FY22 was $6.5B, FY23 was $7.6B, FY24 was $8.1B, and FY25 was $6.5B. Construction-related work leads current spending, with Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction at $585M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $384M. IT and professional services also figure prominently, with Computer Systems Design Services at $335M and Other Computer Related Services at $307M. Top vendors reflect that mix of construction and IT: AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC and GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. each have $132M in obligations, and RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. has $129M. The FY2026 to date snapshot is partial, reflecting fiscal year to date obligations rather than a full-year total.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Interior's contract spending changed recently?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 37.9% year over year. The current fiscal year to date total is $4.1B, down from $6.5B in FY25 and below prior peaks such as $8.1B in FY24. The series shows a rise from $5.1B in FY21 to $8.1B in FY24 before the decline to the current level."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are the largest purchasing categories and who benefits?",
        "body": "Construction categories dominate current obligations, led by Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction at $585M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $384M. IT and professional services account for significant spending as well, with Computer Systems Design Services at $335M and Other Computer Related Services at $307M. Major contractors span construction and IT, with top vendors including AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC at $132M and GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $132M."
      }
    ]
